menu "Observore"

config OBSERVORE_HOSTNAME
    string "Hostname"
    default "observore"
    help
        Used twice.  It is the mDNS name, answering as "<hostname>.local" on
        the link it is joined to.  It is also the hostname offered in the DHCP
        request, which is what a router registers in its own DNS and shows in
        its client list.

        The DHCP one is the more useful of the two across subnets: mDNS is
        link-local multicast and does not route, so a device on an isolated
        VLAN needs the router to reflect mDNS before the .local name reaches
        the main LAN -- whereas ordinary DNS crosses VLANs by design.

        mDNS names are always ".local" (RFC 6762); that suffix is not taken
        from DHCP and must not be changed.

config OBSERVORE_LED_GPIO
    int "User LED GPIO"
    default 27 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C5
    default 21
    help
        GPIO21 is the XIAO ESP32S3 user LED.  GPIO27 is the addressable RGB LED
        on the ESP32-C5-DevKitC-1 and on Waveshare's ESP32-C5-WIFI6-KIT, which
        share a pinout.

choice OBSERVORE_LED_KIND
    prompt "User LED type"
    default OBSERVORE_LED_ADDRESSABLE if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C5
    default OBSERVORE_LED_MONO
    help
        Which kind of LED is on OBSERVORE_LED_GPIO.  Driving an addressable
        pixel as a plain GPIO leaves it dark or stuck on a random colour, and
        driving a plain LED as a pixel does nothing at all, so this has to
        match the board.

config OBSERVORE_LED_MONO
    bool "Single-colour LED on a GPIO"
    help
        One monochrome LED driven directly.  Threat level is a blink rhythm,
        because there is no colour to carry it.

config OBSERVORE_LED_ADDRESSABLE
    bool "Addressable RGB pixel (WS2812)"
    help
        A WS2812 or compatible pixel on a single data line.  Threat level is a
        colour, which is legible at a glance in a way that counting flashes
        never is.

endchoice

config OBSERVORE_LED_ACTIVE_LOW
    bool "The LED is active low"
    depends on OBSERVORE_LED_MONO
    default y
    help
        True when the pin sinks the LED, so driving it low lights it -- which
        is how the XIAO ESP32S3 is wired.  Boards that source the LED from the
        pin want this off, otherwise the LED is lit whenever it should be dark.

config OBSERVORE_LED_BRIGHTNESS
    int "Addressable LED brightness (1-255)"
    depends on OBSERVORE_LED_ADDRESSABLE
    range 1 255
    default 24
    help
        WS2812 pixels are painfully bright at full scale, and this device is
        meant to sit in a room unattended.  The default is dim enough to live
        with overnight and still be obvious in daylight.

config OBSERVORE_BUTTON_GPIO
    int "Mode button GPIO"
    default 28 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C5
    default 0
    help
        The BOOT button, active low: GPIO0 on the XIAO ESP32S3, GPIO28 on the
        ESP32-C5.  Holding it while running toggles the console SoftAP; it only
        enters download mode when held across a reset, so reusing it here is
        safe.

config OBSERVORE_NTP_SERVER
    string "Fallback NTP server"
    default "pool.ntp.org"
    help
        Used only when the network hands out no NTP server over DHCP.

        DHCP is tried first on purpose. This device is meant to sit on the
        segment the cameras are on, and that segment is often firewalled from
        the internet -- the same isolation that stops a push notification
        reaching its server stops a public pool answering. A router's own NTP
        is reachable from inside that fence.

        Times are always UTC on the wire; the console renders local.

comment "Wi-Fi uplink -- leave blank here and set it with menuconfig or NVS"

config OBSERVORE_WIFI_SSID
    string "Home network SSID (optional)"
    default ""
    help
        Leave this EMPTY in sdkconfig.defaults, which is a tracked file.  Set
        it with `idf.py menuconfig` (it lands in sdkconfig, which is
        gitignored), or with a gitignored credentials.conf, or at runtime from
        the console -- which stores it in NVS and never touches the repo at
        all.  A credential set at runtime takes precedence over this one.

        tools/check_no_secrets.sh fails if a value ends up in a tracked file.

config OBSERVORE_WIFI_PASSWORD
    string "Home network password (optional)"
    default ""
    help
        Empty means an open network.  Same warning as the SSID above: never
        commit a real value.

config OBSERVORE_WIFI_AUTOJOIN
    bool "Join the configured network at boot"
    default y
    help
        When credentials are configured, join at boot so the console is
        reachable on your LAN without the SoftAP.

        This costs detection: the radio has one channel, so while associated
        the Wi-Fi sniffer is suspended and only BLE scanning and AP scans
        continue.  Turn this off to stay on full patrol and reach the uplink
        only on demand.  If the network cannot be joined, Observore falls back to
        patrol rather than sitting associated to nothing.

config OBSERVORE_PATROL_WINDOW_S
    int "Seconds spent patrolling before visiting the uplink"
    default 120
    range 20 3600
    help
        Observore is built to sit in one place, and all Wi-Fi detection -- the
        access-point scan and the promiscuous sniff both -- runs only while
        patrolling.  A device parked permanently on the uplink is a BLE-only
        detector, which gives up Remote ID drone and hidden-AP detection
        entirely.  So it alternates: patrol for this long, then join the
        network briefly to push what it found.

menu "Display"

config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY
    bool "Panel over SPI"
    default n
    help
        A 240x320 panel driven through esp_lcd, used in landscape. Off on
        every board that has no panel. Which controller sits behind the glass
        is a separate choice, because the same product ships with more than
        one and a build for one shows garbage on the other.

if OBSERVORE_DISPLAY

choice OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_PANEL
    prompt "Panel controller"
    default OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_ST7789

config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_ST7789
    bool "ST7789"
    help
        The later revisions of the 2.8" ESP32-2432S028R. Verified on the
        bench.

config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_ILI9341
    bool "ILI9341"
    help
        The original 2.8" ESP32-2432S028R and most 2.4" and 3.2" siblings.
        Driven through the espressif/esp_lcd_ili9341 component. Not yet
        confirmed on a bench: the colour order, inversion and mirroring below
        are the values every other project uses for it, and each is a build
        option in case they are wrong.

endchoice


config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_MOSI
    int "MOSI GPIO"
    default 13
config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_MISO
    int "MISO GPIO (-1 if unused)"
    default 12
config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_SCLK
    int "SCLK GPIO"
    default 14
config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_CS
    int "CS GPIO"
    default 15
config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_DC
    int "DC GPIO"
    default 2
config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_RST
    int "Reset GPIO (-1 if wired to EN)"
    default -1
config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_BL
    int "Backlight GPIO (-1 if always on)"
    default 21
config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_MHZ
    int "SPI clock, MHz"
    default 26
    range 1 80
config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_INVERT
    bool "Invert colours"
    default n
    help
        Wrong looks like a photographic negative and is obvious at a glance.
        The ST7789 revision of the 2432S028R on the bench did not need it,
        despite reports that it does; the ILI9341 revision is not expected to
        either.
config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_BGR
    bool "Panel takes BGR order"
    default n
    help
        Wrong swaps red and blue: the alert band comes out blue.
config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_MIRROR_X
    bool "Mirror X"
    default y
config OBSERVORE_DISPLAY_MIRROR_Y
    bool "Mirror Y"
    default n

endif

endmenu

config OBSERVORE_NOTIFIER
    bool "Push notifications"
    default y
    help
        Whether the image can send notifications at all. Off means absent,
        not disabled: the notifier and its queue are not compiled in, and the
        console hides the panel rather than showing a form that cannot work.

        For a device with a display, or one only ever read through the
        console. Every memory problem this project has had was on the uplink
        and most of it was the TLS handshake behind a notification, so a board
        with little RAM and no PSRAM is better off without the code than with
        it switched off. The update check and OTA keep their own TLS path.

config OBSERVORE_BOARD
    string "Board identifier"
    default ""
    help
        Which board this image was built for, using the same names the release
        and the web flasher use: xiao-esp32s3, devkit-esp32c5, xiao-esp32c5,
        xiao-esp32c6.

        This is not the chip. Two of those boards are ESP32-C5s and they are not
        interchangeable -- they differ in LED type and pin, button pin, and
        whether the USB socket is the chip's own peripheral or a UART bridge.
        A browser flashing over USB cannot tell them apart either, which is why
        the installer offers a board picker rather than deciding for you.

        A device updating itself has the same problem and no one to ask, so it
        carries the answer. Left empty, the device will not install an update:
        not knowing which image is yours is a reason to stop, not to guess.

config OBSERVORE_UPDATE_URL
    string "Update manifest URL"
    default "https://observore.forgedinfeatherstechnology.com/firmware/boards.json"
    help
        The document the device reads to learn what the current release is.
        This is the same file the web flasher uses, so a release that is
        installable is by definition one the device can see, and there is no
        second piece of infrastructure to keep in step.

        Point it somewhere else to track your own builds. It is read over
        HTTPS and verified against the certificate bundle, like the notifiers.

config OBSERVORE_UPDATE_ASSET_BASE
    string "Release asset base URL"
    default "https://github.com/Forged-in-Feathers-Technology/observore/releases/download"
    help
        Where the image itself is fetched from. The version comes from
        OBSERVORE_UPDATE_URL; this is joined with it and the board name as
        "<base>/<version>/observore-<board>.bin".

        Deliberately not the same host as the manifest. The flasher site sits
        behind a CDN that compresses this file and then answers range requests
        against the compressed copy while serving the decompressed one: asking
        for bytes 0-4095 returns 10,602 bytes and the reported total is the
        compressed size. An image assembled from those ranges is wrong.

        The release asset host answers ranges correctly, and ranges are what
        make the download affordable -- MBEDTLS_SSL_IN_CONTENT_LEN is 8192 here,
        half the largest TLS record, so an unbounded stream fails on the first
        full-size record with MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_BAD_INPUT_DATA.

config OBSERVORE_UPDATE_CHECK_HOURS
    int "Hours between update checks"
    default 24
    range 1 168
    help
        Checking costs a TLS handshake in an uplink window, which is the most
        memory-constrained moment this device has. Once a day is plenty for
        a project that releases every few weeks, and it keeps that cost away
        from the windows where notifications need the heap.

        Set to 0 hours is not offered: use the console if you want to check
        immediately.

config OBSERVORE_UPLINK_WINDOW_S
    int "Seconds spent on the uplink before returning to patrol"
    default 30
    range 10 3600
    help
        Long enough to flush queued notifications.  The window is held open
        while somebody is actually reading the console, so browsing is never
        cut off mid-page.

config OBSERVORE_UPLINK_MAX_S
    int "Hard ceiling on one uplink visit (seconds)"
    default 180
    range 30 3600
    help
        The uplink window is extended while the console is being read, and the
        page polls every two seconds -- so a browser tab left open would keep
        the device on the uplink indefinitely, where it does no Wi-Fi detection
        at all.  Past this, it returns to patrol regardless.  A user interface
        must not be able to blind the detector.

config OBSERVORE_CONSOLE_IDLE_S
    int "Seconds of console silence before the uplink window may close"
    default 45
    range 5 600

config OBSERVORE_UPLINK_RETRY_S
    int "Backoff after a failed uplink join (seconds)"
    default 300
    range 30 3600
    help
        A join that fails costs the connect timeout, so after a failure the
        next attempt waits this long rather than retrying every patrol window
        and spending a fifteen-second stall each time.  A successful join
        resumes the ordinary alternation.

config OBSERVORE_UPLINK_GRACE_S
    int "Seconds to tolerate a dropped uplink before patrolling"
    default 45
    range 5 600
    help
        Brief drops are normal and reconnect on their own.  Past this, staying
        in uplink mode means the Wi-Fi sniffer is off and there is no network
        either, so patrolling is strictly better.

config OBSERVORE_WIFI_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S
    int "Uplink connect timeout (seconds)"
    default 15
    range 5 60

config OBSERVORE_BLE_SCAN_INTERVAL_MS
    int "BLE scan interval (ms)"
    default 160
    range 20 1000

config OBSERVORE_BLE_SCAN_WINDOW_MS
    int "BLE scan window (ms)"
    default 60
    range 10 1000
    help
        Must not exceed the interval.  BLE and Wi-Fi share one radio and the
        coexistence arbiter divides it by this duty cycle, sharply and
        non-linearly: at a 100% duty cycle the Wi-Fi sniffer receives almost
        nothing at all (2 frames in 40 s, measured), while 37.5% still yields
        around 200.  The default trades roughly a third of BLE throughput for
        a sniffer that functions.

config OBSERVORE_CONSOLE_AUTH
    bool "Require the console password in uplink mode"
    default y
    help
        Without this, anyone who can reach the device on your network can read
        the detection log -- which says what was watching you and when -- and
        change the network it joins.

        The credential is the same generated console password printed on the
        serial log.  It is asked for once and exchanged for a session cookie,
        so the password itself is not repeated on every request; that matters
        because it is also the SoftAP's WPA2 key.

        The SoftAP is not challenged: WPA2 already authenticates that link with
        the same password, and first-time setup is the one moment the console
        is the only way in.

        Turn this off only on a network you would be content to leave the
        detection log readable on.

config OBSERVORE_AP_SSID_PREFIX
    string "Console SoftAP SSID prefix"
    default "console"
    help
        The last three bytes of the MAC are appended.  The default deliberately
        does not advertise what the device is.

config OBSERVORE_AP_PASSWORD
    string "Console SoftAP password override (leave EMPTY)"
    default ""
    help
        Leave this empty.  Each device then generates its own random console
        password on first boot, keeps it in NVS and prints it on the serial
        log -- so no two devices share one, and nothing published in this
        repository reveals it.

        Deriving the password from the MAC would be no better than publishing
        it: the SoftAP's BSSID appears in every Wi-Fi scan.

        Setting a value here overrides that, and every device built from the
        resulting firmware will share the password you choose.  It must be at
        least 8 characters or the console SoftAP falls back to open.

endmenu
